# Inner-Source Contribution Rules

## Proposing a Change

Before writing any code for a non-trivial change, open a GitHub Issue with the title format:

```
[PROPOSAL] {short description}
```

The issue body must include:
- The problem being solved
- The proposed solution
- Which teams are affected

DO NOT open a PR for changes that require platform team approval until the proposal issue has been approved by the platform team. Writing code before approval wastes everyone's review time.

## Branch Naming

Always create branches using the CLI:

```
devex branch {WORK_ID} {description}
```

The Work ID must match `^[A-Z]+-\d+$` (e.g. `PLAT-42`, `FIN-123`).

The branch name produced will be `{WORK_ID}/{description}` (e.g. `PLAT-42/feat/go-stack-support`).

DO NOT create branches manually with `git checkout -b` — use `devex branch` so the Work ID is registered in `.devex/config.yaml` and a `DoraEvent` is emitted.

DO NOT use branch names without a Work ID prefix (e.g. `fix/bug`, `update-docs`).

## PR Requirements

PR title format:

```
{WORK_ID}: {lowercase imperative description}
```

Example: `PLAT-42: add Go stack support`

All of the following must be true before merging:

- PR title starts with a valid Work ID matching `^[A-Z]+-\d+$`
- Two reviewers have approved — at least one must be from the platform team
- All CI tests pass
- `devex check` passes on the branch

DO NOT merge a PR that has fewer than two approvals.

DO NOT merge a PR where `devex check` is failing.

## What Requires Platform Team Approval

The following changes require a `[PROPOSAL]` issue to be approved by the platform team before a PR may be opened:

- Changes to the `DoraEvent` schema (any field addition, removal, or type change)
- Changes to the `LambdaServiceConstruct` public API in `framework/src/`
- New CLI commands added to `cli/src/devex_cli/commands/`
- Changes to the `.devex/config.yaml` schema

## What Teams Can Do Without Approval

The following do not require a proposal issue:

- Adding or improving examples and documentation
- Reporting bugs by opening a GitHub Issue
- Proposing new stack support — the proposal itself does not require pre-approval, but the implementation PR does

DO NOT interpret "no approval needed" as "no review needed" — all PRs still require two reviewers.
